(c) whether such a subjectivity adjudication would at times, deny justice to the relief seeker?

(a) whether provisions of Essential Commodities Act are adequate to enable States to prevent hoarding of wheat and pulses, etc., thereby checking undue price rise;
(b) if so, how many traders have been booked, for crossing the limits imposed by the State Governments since August, 2006 when powers under this Act were invoked and regularly renewed every six months till now State-wise; and
(c) the other steps being taken to prevent any further price-rise of foodgrains meant for vulnerable sections of society?

(a) the number of Indian citizens reported to have been arrested in foreign countries for travelling with false documents, during 2005 and 2006;
(b) whether there is any mechanism to cross check the reported arrests with the families of such persons;
(c) whether any special drive has been launched to meet the twin objective of getting such Indians back to India and simultaneously ensuring that prospectively such a situation is avoided; and 
(d) whether co-operation of NGOs has been sought in this direction?

(a) whether the World Conservation Union put 45 tree species across India on its “Red List of Threatened Plant Species 2007” and has further flagged 247 species as “threatened”;
(b) if so, whether these threatened species are being overused for medicinal or religious purposes without adequate conservation; and
(c) the steps Botanical Survey of India is adopting to preserve forest culture especially in view of alarming situation developing due to global warming also?

(a) whether it is a fact that due to failure to detect a ship, a submarine of the Indian Navy collided with a merchant ship in January, 2008 in the Arabian sea, off the cost of Saurashtra;
(b) if so, was it a human failure or a mechanical defect; and
(c) whether such sensitive submarines are equipped with the latest, state of the art advanced technological developed instruments so as not to compromise the national security and lives of sailors and costly submarines?

(a) whether it is a fact that during the last three years PDS diversion worth Rs. 31,500 crores has taken place;
(b) if so, whether investigations have been made, culprits booked and exemplary punishments given;
(c) whether the Planning Commission had given any report on this sorry state of affairs;
(d) if so, the findings of this report; and
(e) whether any immediate remedial measures were taken to avoid such recurrences?

(a) the steps being taken by Government to expedite the computerization of all the post office branches in the country;
(b) the total branches computerized as of now; and
(c) the reasons for delay in such computerization?

(a) how many P.H.C.’s (Primary Health Centres) have been set-up all over the country and what is being done to ensure the attendance of doctors and medical staff and supply of medicines at these centres;
(b) whether there is any mechanism to ensure proper operation of these centres in all the above aspects; and
(c) if so, the details of the mechanism thereof?

THE MINISTER OF STATE FOR HEALTH & FAMILY WELFARE 
(SMT. PANABAKA LAKSHMI)
(a): As on March, 2007, there are a total of 22370 Primary Health Centres functioning in the country. A Task Group constituted under the National Rural Health Mission under the chairmanship of Director General of Health Services has recommended the following measures to ensure the services of doctors in rural areas : 
-Increase in the age of retirement of doctors to 65 years preferably with posting near hometown;
-Decentralization of recruitment at district level;
-Walk-in-interview and contractual appointment of doctors;
-Enhancing the salary for posting in rural areas by one-third;
-Increasing the admission capacity in medical colleges for Anesthesia;
-Reviving the Diploma Course in Anesthesia;
-To start one year Certificate Course in Anesthesia for Medical Officers working in the system at present to be given by National Board of Examination. 
-Recognition of five hundred bedded Hospitals to provide the facility for conducting the above course;
-Hiring of private practitioners on case-to-case basis.
(b) & ( c ): The PHCs are run by the State/UT Governments. Under National Rural Health Mission[NRHM], funds are released to States/UTs Governments for upgradation and strengthening of Primary Health Centres. The State/UT Governments are however required to incorporate their requirement in their annual NRHM Programme Implementation Plans.

(a) whether Government is aware of the UNAIDS estimates, released in November 2007, that there are 33.2 million people living with HIV worldwide, i.e. 16 per cent less as compared to 39.5 million estimated in 2006;
(b) if so, what are the estimated HIV/ AIDS cases in the country on 31st December, 2007 as compared to 31st December, 2006;
(c) whether it is a fact that many suspected HIV/ AIDS patients really do not know that they are HIV positive; and
(d) the steps being taken to create awareness about this deadly disease?

(a) by when India is going to achieve self-sufficiency in production of the polio vaccine as Government is vigorously pursuing the polio awareness drive all over the country;
(b) whether we are importing the vaccine; and
(c) if so, from where and what is the progress of the indigenous production of the vaccine so that we do not have to rely on the import of the said vaccine for too long?

(a) whether Government is aware that many States in the country are facing a 
major power crisis which, in turn, is hampering their development in a major way;
(b) if so, what efforts are being made in co-ordination with the affected State 
Governments to rectify the situation;
(c) whether a time frame has been set to address the problem; and
(d) if so, the details thereof?

(a) whether it is a fact that DRDO is working on a project aiming to equip future soldiers with light weight force multipliers with miniaturized communication and GPS system, etc., akin to the U.S. Army`s Land Warriors;
(b) if so, whether it is also a fact that the infantryman at present carries a lot of weight and by reducing it, they can perform better in battle and counter insurgency operations; and
(c) if so, by when the DRDO sponsored project is going to be implemented?

(a) the length of the roads constructed till December, 2006 as Cabinet approval for construction of 5,000 km. under the Highway Plan was accorded in December, 2003; 
(b) whether the annual targets fixed for North-South and East-West corridors have been achieved; 
(c) if not, whether there has been any cost-over-run or time-over-run; and
(d) if so, the details thereof?

(a) whether Government are aware that around 1,11,000 qualified Indians work on foreign ships, bulk of which are registered in Panama, western coast countries, etc. and most of them are 25 years old;
(b) if so, whether these vessels being 25 years old and in bad shape, still plying, are being checked/impounded as ships more than 15 years old cannot ferry goods in the sea whenever they come in Indian territorial waters; and
(c) whether any cases of deaths of Indian sailors on these ships have come to the notice of Government and if so, the details during the last three years?

(a) whether the Association of South East Asian Nations (ASEAN) had attended to the sensitivities of India during the negotiations held during January, 2007 in Philippines, for the proposed Free Trade Agreement(FTA);
(b) if so, whether India’s objective of forging a consensus with the ASEAN on the contentious issues of the negative list of items that we did not want to be considered, was adequately achieved; and
(c) whether the interests of Indian farmers and sections of the domestic industry have been safeguarded?

(a) whether Government are aware that despite spending Rs.10 lac per student in IIT/IIM only less than 5 per sent end up doing what they are best trained to do with many of them heading westwards;
(b) if so, whether it is also a fact that India turns out around 16~500 Ph. Ds per annum as compared to 45~000 in US, and a large part of them are of Asian origin particularly from India and China; and
(c) with our economy looking up with meteoric, rise in telecom, IT, BPO and pharma industries, needing large number of professionals, the manner with which this yawning gap would be filled?

(a) whether Government are aware of the observations made by Pakistan’s Minister for Environment, on the sidelines of Delhi Sustainable Development Summit, 2007 held during 4th week of January 2007, that “ India should focus on the environmental aspects of Siachen Glaciers which are melting at a faster rate due to global warming and continuous human traffic owing to heavy deployment of troops;
(b) if so, what is the reaction of Government keeping the security of the nation in view, towards the suggestion of Pakistan’s Minister; and 
(c) whether a comparative study has been made of other similar glaciers regarding the magnitude of melting rate where there is no human traffic?

(a) whether the Merchant Shipping Act, 1958 enables Government to impound a ship registered in a foreign country, when there is a death due to negligence or murder of Indian sailors on such ships; 
(b) if so, the manner in which Government assures that our sailors get due justice whenever any serious physical injury or death comes to their notice suo-moto or is specifically brought to their notice; and 
(c) whether the provisions of this Act of 1958 are adequate to protect interests of Indian sailors or whether any amendment is required?

(a) whether Government are aware that our Naval Chief had said in February, that ‘Indian Ocean region has become the de-facto home of global terrorism with regional powers covertly or overtly, aiding and abetting subversive elements;
(b) if so, whether our avowed aim to become cognizable regional power would be achieved, in the foreseeable future; and
(c) whether a synergy syndrome would be created through collective efforts of all the associated agencies, to quicken this process?

(a) whether Government are aware of the recent findings of the International Labour Organisation that only 36 per cent of employable women are actively engaged in labour market compared to 82.2 per cent men;
(b) if so, whether it is due to lack of suitable opportunities or congenial work environment like low wages, lack of social protection, etc., which keep employable women away from engaging themselves in labour market; and
(c) whether Government purpose to launch special drive to evolve women-friendly engagement culture, through more education, specific training, so that women contribute towards economic growth of the country, and simultaneously raise their standard of living thereby?

(a) whether the Chairman, HDFC Bank has submitted, in February, 2007, a report suggesting setting up a Special Purpose Vehicle abroad, for collecting funds there and then lending to Indian Companies and also to finance import of capital goods used for infrastructure projects;
(b) if so, when and where such a SPV would come into operation; and
(c) whether such collecting of funds from abroad will adversely affect our foreign exchange reserves in future?

(a) whether Government are aware of the “Advanced Micro application of laser welding” which was discussed through presentation of national level papers on this topic by around 44 groups from different State was organized by well-known Vishveshvarya National Institute of Technology in Nagpur in January 2007;
(b) if so, whether Government would encourage research and development activities being undertaken by well-established institutes; and 
(c) whether the existing technologies, system, procedure are upgraded through assimilation/adaptation such R&D?

(a) whether Government are aware that anaemia in pregnant women causes 20 per cent of the infant mortality and that nearly nine out of 10 pregnant women between 15 and 49 years are malnourished and anaemic;
(b) if so, whether Government propose to launch a targeted programme, with active co-operation of the State Governments to rectify this position and what is the time-frame set for the same in the Eleventh Five Year Plan and at what extent the problem would be solved; 
(c) what is the financial outlay per annum for going ahead with such a scheme;
(d) whether any study has been conducted in the past to have a holistic approach towards finding a solution to this sorry state of affairs; and
(e) what are the causes of this state of affairs in the 60th year of Independence?

